              "text": "frīgō, frīgere — \"to fry, to parch\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Coquus piscēs in oleō frīxit. The cook fried the fish in oil."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "frīgō, frīgere, frīxī, frīctum, long ī, in a sartāgō (taught). English fry comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "frīgō is compared by Lewis & Short with Greek phrygō, \"to roast, to parch\". English fry comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
